From: Fathom <fathom@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] x1000: h2boot: the stock updater's signal, and no panic on a late USB response

For Fathom on the HiFi Walker H2 / Eros Q hw4.

On the Q1 and the Rocker, the stock firmware's updater leaves 0x1a1a in
the CPM scratch register and reboots, for the bootloader to start the
stock recovery, which applies update.upt; h2boot ignored it on the Eros
Q, so Firmware Update did nothing with h2boot installed (checked on the
H2). Honour it here too, so the stock updater works with h2boot installed
(going back to stock, or reinstalling), if the Eros Q's stock firmware
uses the same signal (to be checked on the H2).

usb_core panicked on a control response arriving in an unexpected ep0
state. h2boot's first start copies the Bluetooth and Wi-Fi firmware out
of the flash for a few seconds; a computer on the cable enumerates the
bootloader meanwhile, gives up waiting, asks again, and the late answer
to the first request then halted the H2 ("invalid control response
ep_state=2", seen with a Mac waiting for the user to allow the device).
Drop such a response instead: the host retries or resets the bus.
---
--- a/bootloader/x1000/main.c
+++ b/bootloader/x1000/main.c
@@ -64,7 +64,7 @@
      * present, blocking with an "insert SD card" prompt if appropriate. */
     disk_mount_all();
 
-#if defined(SHANLING_Q1) || defined(AGPTEK_ROCKER_NATIVE)
+#if defined(SHANLING_Q1) || defined(AGPTEK_ROCKER_NATIVE) || defined(EROS_QN)
     /* This is needed for OF updates. The OF extracts the update package
      * to the SD card and reboots to the recovery kernel to complete the
      * update. */
--- a/firmware/usbstack/usb_core.c
+++ b/firmware/usbstack/usb_core.c
@@ -1422,7 +1422,10 @@
         }
         break;
     default:
-        panicf("usb_core: invalid control response ep_state=%d", ep0_state);
+        /* A late response to a request already answered (the host gave up
+         * waiting and asked again while the device was busy): drop it. The
+         * host retries or resets the bus; halting the device helps nobody. */
+        logf("usb_core: invalid control response ep_state=%d", ep0_state);
         break;
     }
     restore_irq(oldlevel);
